# =============================================================================
# Inventory — MOVEMENTS LEDGER (VS Code REST Client) — GET /api/v1/stores/{storeId}/inventory/products/{productPublicId}/movements (STORE ADMIN or ADMIN)
# =============================================================================
# Semantics: the pair's full audit trail, chronological (oldest first), no
# pagination (ticket 0012, Q2 B). Every stock mutation writes exactly one
# row; alerting config (threshold) writes none. Movements are system-
# authored: the client never sends type/delta/before/after. The read is
# ARCHIVE-BLIND: after a delist archives the hot row, the ledger stays
# readable — history is the institutional memory that survives. The
# delist's async archive never races this read, by design.
#
# How to run:
#   1. Start the backend: `docker compose up -d --build` — http://localhost:4000
#   2. Click the "Send Request" CodeLens above any request, IN ORDER, in one
#      sitting (captures are session-scoped).
#
# Prerequisites (admin persona):
#   Signup admin@devtunde.com with Password123! once, then promote in MySQL:
#     update users set role = 'ROLE_ADMIN' where email = 'admin@devtunde.com';
#
# Notes:
#   - All data is FAKE (devtunde.com emails, Nigerian numbers).
#   - The automated suites: InventoryApiTests, InventoryLifecycleTests.
#   - Re-runnable: manager, brand, category, SKU and contact emails are minted
#     with {{$timestamp}} — no 409 collisions with prior runs.
#   - Access tokens expire after 15 MINUTES. If tokened requests suddenly 401,
#     re-send the SETUP requests from the top.
#   - REST Client captures die on file/VS Code reopen — re-send the setup
#     requests from the top; never continue on a dead capture.
#   - Expected codes: 200 ledger (never 404 for a stocked pair, even after
#     delist), 401 no/invalid token, 403 wrong identity, 404 unknown/closed
#     store or listed-but-unstocked pair.
# =============================================================================

@baseUrl = http://localhost:4000/api/v1
@authUrl = http://localhost:4000/api/v1/auth

### 1 — Setup: login the admin persona
# Expected: 200
# @name adminLogin
POST {{authUrl}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "admin@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### 2 — Setup: signup the store manager (minted fresh each run)
# Expected: 201
# @name mgrSignup
POST {{authUrl}}/signup
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "Movements Probe Manager",
  "email": "probe-mvmt-mgr-{{$timestamp}}@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!",
  "phone": "+2348031234603"
}

### 3 — Setup: login the manager
# Expected: 200
# @name mgrLogin
POST {{authUrl}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{mgrSignup.response.body.user.email}}",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### 4 — Setup: create the store
# Expected: 201
# @name storeOk
POST {{baseUrl}}/stores
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

{
  "brand": "Movements Probe Store {{$timestamp}}",
  "description": "Probe store for the ledger",
  "storeType": "BRANCH",
  "storeAdminPublicId": "{{mgrLogin.response.body.user.publicId}}",
  "contact": {
    "address": "12 Market Road, Lagos",
    "phone": "08012345678",
    "email": "movements-probe-{{$timestamp}}@devtunde.com"
  }
}

### 5 — Setup: create a category
# Expected: 201
# @name catOk
POST {{baseUrl}}/categories
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

{
  "name": "Movements Category {{$timestamp}}"
}

### 6 — Setup: list a product in the store
# Expected: 201
# @name listingOk
POST {{baseUrl}}/products/store/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

{
  "name": "Movements Milk",
  "sku": "SKU-MV-{{$timestamp}}",
  "description": "Milk for ledger probes",
  "brand": "DairyCo",
  "image": "https://cdn.devtunde.com/milk.png",
  "categoryPublicId": "{{catOk.response.body.publicId}}",
  "mrp": 15000.00,
  "sellingPrice": 12000.00
}

### 7 — Setup: create stock — the ledger's first row (OPENING 0 -> 100)
# Expected: 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

{
  "productPublicId": "{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}",
  "quantity": 100,
  "lowStockThreshold": 10
}

### 8 — A delivery lands (RECEIVED +50 -> 150)
# Expected: 200 — quantity 150
POST {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory/products/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}/received
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

{
  "quantity": 50
}

### 9 — Five crates spoil (DAMAGE −5 -> 145)
# Expected: 200 — quantity 145
POST {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory/products/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}/damage
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

{
  "quantity": 5,
  "reason": "Cold chain broke overnight"
}

### 10 — A count says 60 (ADJUSTMENT −85 -> 60)
# Expected: 200 — quantity 60
PUT {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory/products/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

{
  "quantity": 60
}

### 11 — Read the ledger: the pair's whole life story, chronological
# Expected: 200 — bare array of 4 rows: [0] OPENING (delta 100, before 0,
# after 100), [1] RECEIVED (delta 50, before 100, after 150), [2] DAMAGE
# (delta −5, before 150, after 145, reason carried), [3] ADJUSTMENT
# (delta −85, before 145, after 60)
GET {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory/products/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}/movements
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 12 — Re-tune the threshold (alerting config — NOT a stock mutation)
# Expected: 200 — lowStockThreshold 15; quantity 60 untouched
PUT {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory/products/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}/threshold
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

{
  "lowStockThreshold": 15
}

### 13 — The ledger is unchanged: thresholds write no movements (Q3 — every
# STOCK mutation records; configuration does not)
# Expected: 200 — still exactly 4 rows
GET {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory/products/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}/movements
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 14 — No Authorization header
# Expected: 401
GET {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory/products/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}/movements

### 15 — Garbage token
# Expected: 401
GET {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory/products/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}/movements
Authorization: Bearer not-a-real-jwt

### 16 — Setup: signup the regular persona (fixed — she never holds a seat)
# Expected: 201 (409 if she already exists — either way the account exists)
# @name regularSignup
POST {{authUrl}}/signup
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "Bola Storeless",
  "email": "bola.storeless@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!",
  "phone": "+2348031234571"
}

### 17 — Setup: login the regular persona (fixed — she never holds a seat)
# Expected: 200
# @name regularLogin
POST {{authUrl}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "bola.storeless@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### 18 — Read the ledger as a regular user
# Expected: 403 — identity gate
GET {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory/products/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}/movements
Authorization: Bearer {{regularLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 19 — Unknown store
# Expected: 404 — title "Store not found"
GET {{baseUrl}}/stores/22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222/inventory/products/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}/movements
Authorization: Bearer {{adminLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 20 — Setup: list a second product but never stock it
# Expected: 201
# @name listingUnstocked
POST {{baseUrl}}/products/store/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

{
  "name": "Unstocked Movements Milk",
  "sku": "SKU-MU-{{$timestamp}}",
  "description": "Listed, shelf never counted",
  "brand": "DairyCo",
  "image": "https://cdn.devtunde.com/milk.png",
  "categoryPublicId": "{{catOk.response.body.publicId}}",
  "mrp": 8000.00,
  "sellingPrice": 7500.00
}

### 21 — Listed but unstocked: no history to read
# Expected: 404 — title "Inventory not found"
GET {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory/products/{{listingUnstocked.response.body.publicId}}/movements
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 22 — Delist the product (the one closing path; the async listener
# archives the hot row — but the ledger read below ignores archived state)
# Expected: 204
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/products/store/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 23 — The ledger survives the delist — archive hides the hot row, never
# the history (Q2 B: the audit story)
# Expected: 200 — still the 4 rows; the read is archive-blind by design
GET {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory/products/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}/movements
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 24 — Close the store, then read the ledger
# Expected: 204
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 25 — Closed store: the ledger is gone with the store
# Expected: 404 — title "Store not found"
GET {{baseUrl}}/stores/{{storeOk.response.body.publicId}}/inventory/products/{{listingOk.response.body.publicId}}/movements
Authorization: Bearer {{mgrLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
